# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — never commit these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real environment files, including .env.local / .env.production.
.env
.env.*
# The two rules above only match a *leading* dot — `prod.env` and `local.env`
# match neither, and are exactly what someone reaches for when juggling two
# deployments.
*.env

# `.env.example` is deliberately NOT re-included here.
#
# It holds only placeholders and is the usual thing to commit, but this project
# keeps it local: `.env.*` already covers it, so there is no negation. The
# variables it documents are listed in README.md instead, which is what a fresh
# clone reads — keep the two in step when adding one.

# direnv. Holds `export` lines, so it is an environment file wearing a
# different name.
.envrc

# Auth.js writes AUTH_SECRET here when you run `npx auth secret`.
.auth

# npm registry auth tokens live here as `//registry…:_authToken=`. A token-free
# .npmrc is a perfectly ordinary thing to commit — force-add it (`git add -f`)
# if this project ever grows one.
.npmrc

# VS Code's Local History extension snapshots every file it sees into the
# workspace, .env included. Ignoring the directory is what stops a copy of the
# secrets walking around the rule above.
.history/

# Cloud CLI credentials, if one is ever run with the project as its home.
.aws/
.gcloud/
.azure/

# Anything deliberately named as such.
/secrets/
*.secrets.*

# Keys and certificates
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.jks
*.keystore
# Apple push/sign-in keys, and anything armoured by GPG.
*.p8
*.asc
*.gpg
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
# Service-account credentials
**/*service-account*.json
**/*credentials*.json
gha-creds-*.json

# Database dumps and local database files.
#
# A dump is as sensitive as the database it came from — customer addresses,
# order history and password hashes, in one file that looks like source.
*.sql
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.dump
*.bak
/prisma-dev-backup*

# Machine-local agent settings: local permission allowlists and absolute paths
# from one person's disk. The shared `settings.json` beside it stays tracked.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.local.json

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated code — recreated by `prisma generate` (runs on postinstall).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/src/generated/

# Uploaded product images — user content, not source. Keep the directory so a
# fresh clone has somewhere to write.
/public/uploads/*
!/public/uploads/.gitkeep

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored AI-assistant skills — reinstalled, not committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma's own reference docs, fetched by `npx @prisma/cli agent install` from
# the `prisma/skills` repo. Not this project's code, and nothing imports them.
#
# `.agents/skills` holds the real files; `.claude/skills` and `.windsurf/skills`
# are per-tool mirrors of it. On Windows those mirrors are symlinks that git
# resolves and copies, so committing all three stored the same 69 files three
# times — 207 files of third-party documentation.
#
# `skills-lock.json` stays tracked, and is what makes them reproducible: it
# records the source and a content hash per skill. Restore them on a fresh
# clone with:
#
#   npx @prisma/cli agent install
#
.agents/skills/
.claude/skills/
.windsurf/skills/

# Claude Code — local agent config, not part of the project.
CLAUDE.md
.claude/

# `AGENTS.md` is the same thing under a tool-neutral name — CLAUDE.md here is
# only a one-line `@AGENTS.md` include. The content is boilerplate regenerated
# by the Next.js agent tooling, so it carries no project knowledge worth
# keeping in the history.
AGENTS.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development records — kept locally, not in the repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session reports, the build report and the long-form work report. They are
# point-in-time accounts of *why* things were done, written for whoever is
# working on this next — not user documentation, and not something a fresh
# clone needs in order to run the project.
#
# The files stay on disk; they were untracked with `git rm -r --cached docs`,
# which is what an ignore rule cannot do on its own. Earlier revisions remain in
# the history. Root README.md is the entry point a clone reads instead.
/docs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-local artefacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Thumbs.db` is listed above; this is the other half of the pair. Explorer
# writes it into any folder whose view settings are touched, so it appears
# beside source rather than only in image directories.
desktop.ini

# Turbopack's cache. `next dev` and `next build` both run through it here, so
# this fills up during ordinary work.
.turbo/

# Editor state. These carry absolute paths from one person's disk, which is
# what makes them a conflict on every pull rather than merely noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# The `*-debug.log` rules above only cover the package manager's own crash
# logs. An application log is the one that ends up holding session tokens and
# customer addresses.
*.log
logs/

# Caches and process state.
.eslintcache
.pnpm-store/
*.pid

# `/node_modules` above is anchored to the repository root, so it does not
# match a nested install (`src/node_modules`, or one inside a workspace).
node_modules/

# Test and build artefacts, including screenshots — Playwright reports embed
# full-page captures of whatever data the run happened to render.
test-results/
playwright-report/
storybook-static/
/out/
